<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "blog", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %> &middot; 27 November 2026</p>
  <h1>Why does the Go hear FLARM but transmit only ADS-L?</h1>
  <p class="lede">A skyBlip Go can show a nearby glider from its FLARM signal. If the glider&rsquo;s FLARM does not receive ADS-L, it cannot show the Go in return. We chose to send our position only in ADS-L, a public radio protocol. That leaves a gap for glider pilots today.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The question from SoftRF_community</h2>
  <h3>Seeing a glider does not mean its FLARM sees you.</h3>
  <p><a href="https://groups.google.com/g/softrf_community/c/-YdkcBhyub8">Moshe Braner pointed out</a> that most gliders carry FLARM. For a pilot counting on those units to see them, a device that never sends FLARM is a hard sell. A PowerFLARM that receives ADS-B on 1090&nbsp;MHz still cannot pick up a Go on that frequency.</p>
  <p>Transmitting FLARM takes a licence from FLARM. We receive it but never transmit it. FLARM says most devices with one radio receive ADS-L poorly or not at all. They switch between channels, and FLARM gives its own signals priority. FLARM sells a licence to <em>transmit</em> ADS-L on PowerFLARM devices and includes transmission on the new PowerFLARM Flex. We hope pilots switch on ADS-L reception where their units support it, and that FLARM makes reception the default. Our <%= link_to_page "blog/who-sees-ads-l", label: "guide to who sees ADS-L" %> covers the other receivers.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Why we transmit ADS-L</h2>
  <h3>We want every device to speak the same public protocol.</h3>
  <p>François built skyBlip first for light powered aircraft, which is what he flies. <a href="https://groups.google.com/g/softrf_community/c/-YdkcBhyub8">In his answer to Moshe</a>, he explained why we chose one way to transmit. The European Union Aviation Safety Agency (EASA) brought manufacturers together around ADS-L and published the specification, ADS-L 4 SRD-860 Issue 2. EASA lists ADS-L as a way to make aircraft electronically conspicuous in U-space under SERA.6005(c).</p>
  <p>Only the protocol needs to be public. Manufacturers can share a radio format and still compete on the products they build. Each device that sends ADS-L instead of a closed protocol gives every ADS-L receiver another signal it can read.</p>
  <p>skyBlip has held its own manufacturer address range in the ADS-L registry since October 2026. Every clause of the specification that the firmware implements has its own conformance test. We are preparing the <%= link_to_page "ads-l/conformity", label: "declaration of conformity" %>.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">One radio on the Go</h2>
  <h3>Extra protocols would have to share the same second.</h3>
  <p>Moshe&rsquo;s SoftRF fork shares one radio among up to four protocols: ADS-L, FLARM, FANET (used by paragliders) and PilotAware (PAW). In the version he was finishing for the SenseCAP MeshTracker X1, ADS-B reception takes the place of PilotAware. His schedule accounts for FLARM&rsquo;s time slots and for how infrequently FANET transmits.</p>
  <p>One radio can listen on only one channel at a time. Adding another protocol takes time away from listening or transmitting on the others. We build the whole second around receiving ADS-L and FLARM and transmitting ADS-L. Go does not receive FANET, PilotAware or the OGN trackers&rsquo; own format.</p>
  <p>Go uses three <%= link_to_page "timing", label: "listening windows", anchor: "second" %>. Count from the start of each UTC second:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>210 to 440&nbsp;ms:</strong> Go listens on 869.525&nbsp;MHz for the ADS-L uplink, the messages sent by ground stations.</li>
    <li><strong>450 to 820&nbsp;ms:</strong> Go listens on 868.2&nbsp;MHz for ADS-L and FLARM from aircraft.</li>
    <li><strong>830 to 1200&nbsp;ms:</strong> Go listens for the same traffic on 868.4&nbsp;MHz. It stays on that channel for 200&nbsp;ms into the next second to hear FLARM-generation devices still transmitting there.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In flight, Go schedules one position each second, on 868.2&nbsp;MHz in even seconds and 868.4&nbsp;MHz in odd ones. It also schedules its callsign and a Status message about every 10&nbsp;s. Status tells other receivers what the Go can receive. The <%= link_to_page "radio", label: "radio guide" %> shows what each window carries.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">A separate radio for ADS-B</h2>
  <h3>Bravo listens on 1090&nbsp;MHz without taking time from Go.</h3>
  <p><%= link_to_page "skyblip-bravo", label: "skyBlip Bravo" %> runs on the SenseCAP MeshTracker X1 and uses its own LR2021 radio to receive ADS-B. Bravo can send ADS-B traffic straight to a navigation app. Pair it with a Go and you have a <%= link_to_page "skyblip-tango", label: "skyBlip Tango" %>, with ADS-L, FLARM and ADS-B traffic on one radar. Go keeps the same transmission and listening schedule.</p>
  <p>Bravo also detects Mode S transponders that do not send ADS-B. Those signals carry no position. Bravo does not report those detections today. We are measuring how signal strength relates to distance before reporting them.</p>
</section>

